study_type: Modular agent-based RAG framework with experimental evaluation
RQ1 orientation: Cyber-defense
RQ1 task_stage: Real-time cyber-attack classification, explanation, and structured reporting; suggested mitigation steps
RQ2 architecture: Central core LLM agent orchestrates specialized classifiers, RAG and report generation; iterative retrieval and reassessment using three in-memory vector stores, with interactive user chat
RQ2 adaptation: Attack-specific supervised classifier fine-tuning; contextual prompting and iterative RAG with Maximal Marginal Relevance retrieval
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: yes; SSTI dataset downloadable by password at reference [44], URL not stated in supplied text

id: R0448
title: Towards Automated Penetration Testing: Introducing LLM Benchmark, Analysis, and Improvements
study_type: Open benchmark for LLM-based automated penetration testing; PentestGPT evaluation and ablation studies
RQ1 orientation: Ethical hacking or penetration testing: simulating cyberattacks to identify system vulnerabilities
RQ1 task_stage: Reconnaissance/enumeration, general techniques, exploitation, and privilege escalation
RQ2 architecture: PentestGPT multi-agent approach with summarizing, reasoning, and task-explanation modules; ablations add summary of summaries, ReAct tool-based structured todo lists, and retrieval context
RQ2 adaptation: Default PentestGPT prompts for GPT-4o; modified prompts for Llama; summary injection, structured generation, and RAG over HackTricks using bge-large, ChromaDB, and bge-reranker
RQ4 code_available: yes https://github.com/isamu-isozaki/PentestGPT
RQ4 data_available: yes https://github.com/isamuisozaki/AI-Pentest-Benchmark

id: R0451
title: Large Language Models are Autonomous Cyber Defenders
study_type: Study of LLMs in multi-agent ACD environments; integration framework and experimental evaluation
RQ1 orientation: Defensive
RQ1 task_stage: Autonomous incident response; detect and mitigate attacks while maintaining service availability
RQ2 architecture: Multi-agent teams: all LLM, all RL, or 1 LLM + 4 RL defenders; per-step natural-language observations, action-and-reason JSON responses and 8-bit communication vectors
RQ2 adaptation: Instructional, few-shot and role prompting; selected cybersecurity-expert role with few-shot examples and refined action definitions
RQ4 code_available: yes https://github.com/r4wd3r/llms-are-acd
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0477
title: ChatSOC: A Large Language Model Powered Autonomous Agent for Security Operations Center
study_type: Practical autonomous agent implemented and deployed at IHEP; functionality demonstration
RQ1 orientation: Defensive: defending against cyberattacks
RQ1 task_stage: Identification, protection, detection, and response; vulnerability scanning, attack detection, IP blocking, notifications, and incident reporting
RQ2 architecture: An autonomous agent with scheduling, memory databases, and security tool interfaces; iterative thought, action, and observation with task decomposition and tool invocation
RQ2 adaptation: Chain-of-thought prompting for task planning; retrieval-augmented generation using a vector database and historical subtasks/results
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0480
title: FORGE: Self-Evolving Agent Memory with No Weight Updates via Population Broadcast
study_type: Population-based, gradient-free self-improvement protocol with evaluation on CybORG CAGE-2
RQ1 orientation: Defensive; blue defender against an automated red attacker
RQ1 task_stage: Network defense; selects defense actions and interprets host-level observations
RQ2 architecture: Hierarchical ReAct Planner with on-demand Analyst and ActionChooser sub-agents; Reflector/Exemplifier evolves persistent prompt-injected memory across 10 parallel instances using champion broadcast and graduation
RQ2 adaptation: Prompt-only memory evolution without weight updates; failure-triggered Reflexion generates Rules, Examples, or Mixed artifacts
RQ4 code_available: yes + https://github.com/isbogdanov/forge-protocol
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0484
title: Context, Reasoning, and Hierarchy: A Cost-Performance Study of Compound LLM Agent Design in an Adversarial POMDP
study_type: Controlled empirical cost-performance study of compound LLM agent design; static architectural design-space study.
RQ1 orientation: Defensive; autonomous cyber defense.
RQ1 task_stage: Network defense using Monitor, Analyse, Remove, Restore, and Decoy actions.
RQ2 architecture: Monolithic ReAct Planner or fixed three-agent hierarchy: Planner, Analyst, ActionChooser; deterministic state tracking and compressed history, optional intra-step deliberation tools.
RQ2 adaptation: Context engineering and identical role/action-reference prompts; optional self-questioning, self-critique, self-improvement and chain-of-thought prompting; no per-model tuning.
RQ4 code_available: yes + https://github.com/isbogdanov/agent-design-study
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0485
title: An LLM-Based Agentic Network Traffic Incident-Report Approach Towards Explainable-AI Network Defense
study_type: Graph-based multi-agent framework with experimental validation
RQ1 orientation: Defensive: network defense and intrusion detection
RQ1 task_stage: Benign/malicious pre-detection, multi-class attack classification, SHAP feature attribution, and evidence-grounded incident-report generation with mitigation recommendations
RQ2 architecture: Graph-based multi-agent pipeline with centralized shared state: Random Forest pre-detection, MLP/RF/XGBoost soft-voting ensemble with SHAP, and LangGraph ReAct summary agent; Chroma DB historical knowledge, Tavily Web Search, and ArXiv Search
RQ2 adaptation: Retrieval-Augmented Generation; classification results, SHAP attributions, and network features injected into prompts; iterative retrieval and reasoning
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0496
title: Exploring Traffic Simulation and Cybersecurity Strategies Using Large Language Models
study_type: Multi-agent framework with a case study
RQ1 orientation: Both cyberattack and defense strategies
RQ1 task_stage: Traffic simulation and cybersecurity testing; design and implementation of cyberattacks, countermeasure development, and impact evaluation
RQ2 architecture: AutoGen multi-agent system; simulation, hacker, defender, and evaluator agents, with coordinated communication and iterative feedback
RQ2 adaptation: Task-specific prompts; iterative code generation for the defense agent
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0528
title: Neuro-Agentic Control: A Deep Learning-Based LLM-Powered Agentic AI Framework for Controlling Security Controls
study_type: Neuro-Agentic Control framework with experimental evaluation
RQ1 orientation: Autonomous defense
RQ1 task_stage: Anomaly detection; counterfactual control strategy formulation and physics-grounded intervention selection
RQ2 architecture: Two-stage control system: Gemini Architect proposes three strategies using retrieved manuals; TimesFM Sentinel forecasts, simulates counterfactuals, and selects or rejects actions in a closed loop
RQ2 adaptation: RAG-enhanced prompting with operational manuals and control constraints; frozen, pre-trained TimesFM used for inference without fine-tuning
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0538
title: SoK: Honeypots & LLMs, More Than the Sum of Their Parts?
study_type: Systematization of Knowledge (SoK); systematic review of LLM-powered honeypots, honeypot fingerprinting, and honeypot data analysis
RQ1 orientation: Defensive
RQ1 task_stage: Cyber deception, occupying attacker resources, and gathering threat intelligence; automated mapping to MITRE ATT&CK and real-time detection
RQ2 architecture: Synthesized canonical architecture: attacker-facing server, filter/router, deterministic responder, prompt creator, LLM, system state manager, and session history curator
RQ2 adaptation: Reviewed approaches include prompting, chain-of-thought prompting, RAG, and protocol-specific fine-tuning; LoRA heads proposed
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0541
title: Towards Trustworthy Agentic IoEV: AI Agents for Explainable Cyberthreat Mitigation and State Analytics
study_type: Agentic AI framework with experimental evaluation
RQ1 orientation: Autonomous cyber-threat defense
RQ1 task_stage: Cyber-threat detection and response at charging stations; explainable threat mitigation and incident summaries
RQ2 architecture: Distributed, tool-augmented, explainable multi-agent system: Safety and Security Agent (SSA), Personalized Support Agent (PSA), Contextualizer Agent (CA), and Solver Agent (SA); shared explainable reasoning layer
RQ2 adaptation: Prompt engineering and RAG for SSA; fine-tuned DistilBERT, pretrained ML/DL tools, and continual learning through federated, transfer, and split learning
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0556
title: Debate-Driven Multi-Agent LLMs for Phishing Email Detection
study_type: Multi-agent large language model debate framework with experiments on five benchmark phishing datasets
RQ1 orientation: not stated
RQ1 task_stage: Phishing email detection; binary classification as phishing or legitimate
RQ2 architecture: Two LLM debater agents arguing opposing positions over two scripted rounds; third judge agent evaluates four logged arguments and issues a verdict with reasoning
RQ2 adaptation: Template prompting; chain-of-thought prompting, role prompting, and their combination evaluated
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0567
title: RAVEN: Retrieval-Augmented Vulnerability Exploration Network for Memory Corruption Analysis in User Code and Binary Programs
study_type: Multi-agent framework with experiments on NIST-SARD
RQ1 orientation: not stated
RQ1 task_stage: Vulnerability identification and CWE classification; root cause, impact and exploitation assessment, report generation and remediation guidance
RQ2 architecture: Four-phase multi-agent workflow: Explorer, Analyst, Reporter and Judge; code ingestion and RAG retrieval using ChromaDB
RQ2 adaptation: RAG with flat, contextual or HyPE chunking; embedding, hybrid or HyPE retrieval; cross-encoder or prompted LLM reranking
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0582
title: Towards Agentic Investigation of Security Alerts
study_type: Experimental agentic security investigation workflow; proof-of-concept with evaluation
RQ1 orientation: Assisting security analysts in alert handling and incident investigation
RQ1 task_stage: Early-stage security alert investigation; contextualise alerts and identify true and false positives
RQ2 architecture: Three distinct prompted LLM components: Investigator, Summary and Incident Verdict; iterative SQL/grep investigation loop limited to two executions
RQ2 adaptation: Basic prompts and context engineering; role instructions, examples and predefined queries
RQ4 code_available: yes + https://github.com/Rub3cula/CyberHunt2025/ (experiment prompts and queries)
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0621
title: Hallucination-Resistant Security Planning with a Large Language Model
study_type: Framework with theoretical analysis and experimental evaluation on an incident response use case.
RQ1 orientation: not stated
RQ1 task_stage: Incident response planning; generating response and recovery actions from system logs and security alerts.
RQ2 architecture: An LLM in an iterative verification and refinement loop: candidate actions, lookahead predictions, consistency evaluation, conformal abstention and external feedback; previous actions and feedback retained in context.
RQ2 adaptation: Fine-tuning, chain-of-thought prompting and in-context learning from external feedback without changing underlying parameters.
RQ4 code_available: yes; https://github.com/Kim-Hammar/csle
RQ4 data_available: yes; https://huggingface.co/datasets/kimhammar/CSLE-IncidentResponse-V1

id: R0698
title: Towards AI-Driven Human-Machine Co-Teaming for Adaptive and Agile Cyber Security Operation Centers
study_type: Vision and proposed framework for AI-driven human-machine co-teaming in SOCs
RQ1 orientation: Defending organizations against evolving cyber threats
RQ1 task_stage: Threat intelligence, alert triage, and incident response; context-aware investigation and remediation
RQ2 architecture: LLM-based AI apprentice collaborating with SOC analysts; four modules for data integration, situational awareness, adaptive decision-making, and explainable AI/plan execution, with a multi-graph model and feedback learning
RQ2 adaptation: Prompt engineering, in-context learning, Chain of Thought prompting, supervised fine-tuning, generative feedback, RLHF, and RAG; learning signals from op traces and ethnographic fieldnotes
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0701
title: Cochise: A Reference Harness for Autonomous Penetration Testing
study_type: Reference harness and prototype with compactness, capability, and analyzability evaluation
RQ1 orientation: Offensive security research
RQ1 task_stage: Autonomous penetration testing; enumerate and compromise Active Directory domains
RQ2 architecture: Planner–Executor architecture: planner maintains a Pentest-Task-Tree and long-term state; per-task ReAct executor issues SSH commands, with shared knowledge component and Reflexion error repair
RQ2 adaptation: Configurable scenario prompt; ReAct and Reflexion patterns
RQ4 code_available: yes + https://github.com/andreashappe/cochise
RQ4 data_available: yes + https://github.com/andreashappe/cochise

id: R0703
title: A Unified Framework for Human–AI Collaboration in Security Operations Centers with Trusted Autonomy
study_type: Human–AI Collaboration Framework; AI-Avatar case study in a simulated cyber range
RQ1 orientation: Defensive; SOC described as a proactive defensive hub
RQ1 task_stage: Monitoring, protection, threat detection, alert triage, and incident response
RQ2 architecture: Five autonomy levels mapped to HITL roles and task-specific trust thresholds; ChatGPT-based AI-Avatar with RAG, knowledge graphs, SIEM, IDS, and firewalls
RQ2 adaptation: Fine-tuning, RAG, and knowledge graphs; training with wargaming, event data, and Red/Blue Team exercises
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0712
title: Can LLMs Hack Enterprise Networks? — RCR Report
study_type: Replicated Computational Results (RCR) Report; artifacts, evaluation setup and analysis scripts
RQ1 orientation: Offensive security capability evaluation
RQ1 task_stage: Autonomous assumed-breach penetration-testing; identifying vulnerabilities and compromising user accounts
RQ2 architecture: High-level Planner creates plans and selects tasks; low-level Executor executes tool calls through a Kali Linux VM via SSH
RQ2 adaptation: Prompts and tool calls; temperature 0 where supported, default temperature for o1
RQ4 code_available: yes https://github.com/andreashappe/cochise
RQ4 data_available: yes https://github.com/andreashappe/cochise/tree/main/examples/initial-paper ; https://zenodo.org/records/17456062

id: R0730
title: Automated Penetration Testing using Large Language Models
study_type: Automated penetration testing system; implementation results and comparative performance analysis
RQ1 orientation: Simulates cyber-attacks to identify vulnerabilities and enhance network security
RQ1 task_stage: Automated penetration testing, vulnerability detection, and suggested remediation; SQL injection and cross-site scripting testing
RQ2 architecture: LLM AI core, command processor, and integration with Nmap, Metasploit, Wireshark, and custom scripts; execution-feedback loop with stored test results and knowledge-base updates
RQ2 adaptation: Fine-tuned for cybersecurity applications; reinforcement learning from execution feedback to adjust command generation
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0735
title: Agentic AI and Large Language Models for Autonomous IoT Cybersecurity: A Systematic Survey, Taxonomy, and Research Roadmap
study_type: PRISMA 2020 systematic survey, taxonomy, and research roadmap; companion reproducibility scaffolding
RQ1 orientation: Autonomous IoT defense; also surveys autonomous penetration testing and vulnerability discovery
RQ1 task_stage: Detection, response, threat hunting, vulnerability discovery, deception; surveyed literature
RQ2 architecture: Surveys single-agent, multi-agent, hybrid and hierarchical designs; kit includes single-agent ReAct and multi-agent coordination loops
RQ2 adaptation: Surveys CoT prompting, ReAct, plan-and-solve, reflection, RAG, fine-tuning, and federated learning; kit provides prompt templates
RQ4 code_available: yes + https://github.com/vnageshwaran-de/agentic-iot-security ; https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20726552
RQ4 data_available: yes + https://github.com/vnageshwaran-de/agentic-iot-security (coded corpus, extraction matrix, codebook, and screening protocol)

id: R0759
title: Generative AI and LLMs for Critical Infrastructure Protection: Evaluation Benchmarks, Agentic AI, Challenges, and Opportunities
study_type: Comprehensive review of AI-driven approaches for Critical Infrastructure Protection.
RQ1 orientation: Critical Infrastructure Protection and proactive defense; also reviews offensive security benchmarks.
RQ1 task_stage: Reviews threat detection, vulnerability detection and mitigation, cyber-attack attribution, and proactive defense mechanisms.
RQ2 architecture: not stated
RQ2 adaptation: Reviews zero-shot and few-shot prompting, Retrieval-Augmented Generation, instruction-tuning, and Feedback-Driven Security Patching using static code analysis.
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0014
title: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity: A Tutorial and Survey of Automated Vulnerability Discovery
study_type: Tutorial and survey; step-by-step walkthrough of agentic penetration testing
RQ1 orientation: Primarily offensive and evaluative agents for vulnerability discovery
RQ1 task_stage: Automated vulnerability discovery and penetration testing; source code auditing, fuzzing, and network security assessment
RQ2 architecture: Surveys single- and multi-agent designs, including Planner–Executor and hierarchical task-specific agents; Observe–Think–Act loops, memory, and tool-use interfaces
RQ2 adaptation: Surveys prompt engineering, CoT, ReAct, Plan-then-Execute, RAG, instruction-tuning, and incremental fine-tuning
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: not stated

id: R0740
title: LLM-Based Agents for Cybersecurity: A Systematic Review of Architectures, Applications, and Open Challenges
study_type: Systematic literature review following PRISMA 2020; five-dimensional taxonomy of 59 primary studies
RQ1 orientation: Both defensive security tasks and penetration testing and red teaming
RQ1 task_stage: Threat intelligence, vulnerability assessment, DevSecOps and SOC automation, intrusion detection; penetration testing, incident response and forensics, compliance and risk assessment, phishing and social engineering
RQ2 architecture: Reviews three single-agent and three multi-agent patterns; tool-calling, plan-and-execute, centralized/hierarchical, pipeline, and debate/consensus
RQ2 adaptation: Screening prompted with explicit inclusion and exclusion criteria; reviewed augmentation strategies include ICL, RAG, fine-tuning, and hybrid
RQ4 code_available: not stated
RQ4 data_available: Full query record, per-paper coding matrix, review protocol, and screening decisions stated as published with the paper; repository URL not stated in supplied text

